 ### Number settings: what is accepted
 
 A number setting is plain ASCII text: digits with an optional sign (whole numbers), or digits with an optional point and exponent (seconds and money). Spaces, tabs and line ends around the value are ignored (in the Windows command prompt, `set DESK_PORT=8765 ` with a trailing space works). A space inside the number, underscores, digits of other scripts (and a no-break space), an empty value, `nan`, `inf`, an exponent or a point in a whole number, and text such as `0x10` are refused. A value outside its range is refused too, with a message that names the setting (never the value) and the desk ends with exit code 4. The ceilings are named constants in `src/lead_desk/constants.py`. An address setting (`DESK_FAKES_URL`, `DESK_LIVE_BASE_URL`, `DESK_HUBSPOT_BASE_URL`) that cannot be read as an address, such as `http://[/`, is refused the same way ("... is not a valid address"). The 600-second ceiling of `DESK_SEND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` and `DESK_SMTP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` is only a sanity bound. Values above 9 are accepted, but the desk cuts every send at 9 seconds.
